00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.130
So, why are we talking
about this today?

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.780
Well, what we are
also seeing is as more

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.130
and more enterprises
are becoming API first,

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.320
they have begun to
move to the cloud

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.990
and are also leveraging a
variety of different kind

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.660
of cloud services.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.420
So cloud services are great.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.690
They give you the
ability to be able to use

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.880
some capability right away.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.920
At the same time, they do
require a specific mechanism

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.850
to interact with them.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:10.080
So on one hand, they do
offer you the entities

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.940
to be able to innovate faster.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.310
But you also need to
be able to use them

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.380
in a more efficient way.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.610
So let's quickly break
down the anatomy of how

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.840
you access a cloud service.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.250
Now, I'm going to use an example
for a Google Cloud service

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.150
in general.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.460
But this could very
well and equally

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.830
apply to pretty much any
of the cloud service.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.760
So, if you think about
a very typical access

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.920
pattern for any cloud
service, or even

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.910
your own service for
that matter of fact,

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.180
it involves a few key steps.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.210
The very first thing you do
as an application developer--

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.030
by the way, how many application
developers or developers

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.720
in the house out here today?

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:51.650
Oh, excellent.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:52.150
Excellent.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.010
So there'll be a
lot more goodness

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.030
for you to hear about today.

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.780
So, the very first thing
as a developer that you do

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.860
is figure out, where are my
credentials to that service

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.170
that I want to access?

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:05.710
That's step one.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:08.440
Then you got to go manage
how you store them.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:13.000
Second, once I have access
to those credentials, then

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.370
usually, most
likely, you're going

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.680
to hit some kind
of a token endpoint

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.430
where you exchange that
credential for an access token.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.542
That token is supposedly
going to be timestamped,

00:04:26.542 --> 00:04:28.750
and which means you have to
manage the life cycle of,

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.170
is the token good, do I need to
refresh it, so on and so forth.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:34.970
And, once you do
all the three steps,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.990
finally, you actually
make a call to the API

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.570
that you desire to
invoke, which means

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:43.300
there's a lot of nanosteps.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:47.420
There's a lot of redundancy
that you have to account for.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.310
It is error prone.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:49.720
What if you miss a step?

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.030
And, more importantly,
this is a common thing

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:55.840
which compliance teams and
administrators do not like.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.930
They don't want developers
touching or messing

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:00.620
with any credentials.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:03.850
That's a domain they want
developers to stay away from.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:09.100
How many developers out here
have to work with credentials?

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.060
OK.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.350
Just keep your hands up,
please, for a second.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.730
How many out there never get
told by a compliance team

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:18.280
about anything at all?

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.660
Are they totally OK with that?

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.760
If they are, bring
your hands down.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:23.540
OK.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.760
So this is a very
common problem, right?

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.580
Compliance is usually
behind clean separation

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:35.240
of credentials, provisioning--
that's all compliance and ops.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.210
Using certain capabilities--
that's developers.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:41.600
So then let's take a
look at how Apigee,

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.130
which is a full lifecycle
API management platform,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:50.630
can really help you develop
these APIs by leveraging some

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:53.250
of these newer cloud services.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:56.180
So what you see out here
within the picture out here

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:00.170
is Apigee API platform,
also known as Apigee Edge.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:05.240
It has full-feature capability
to have out-of-the-box policies

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:06.980
to support your
security requirements,

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.180
traffic management,
transformation,

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.200
so on and so forth.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.800
As you start to go up
in your API program,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.280
you want to ensure that you
have enough capabilities

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.160
to be able to expose your API
so they can be consumed easily

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.900
by developers.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.895
And then the analytics give
you that clean insight,

00:06:24.895 --> 00:06:27.240
and not only from an
operational perspective,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.050
but also from metrics, which
cater to different stakeholders

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.940
such as, are the
APIs being used?

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.670
Who is using them, right?

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:36.880
So on and so forth.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.200
And now, extensions is
a very useful capability

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.210
that we added--

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.220
when GA October of last year.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.610
It was announced at Google
Cloud Next in London last year.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.470
And today, we are
pleased to announce

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.920
some really cool extensions
that we have made

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.390
available to our customers.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:59.730
But because extensions become
part of the core platform,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:03.720
you, as a developer who's
leveraging the Apigee platform,

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.540
can leverage all the
entities and connect

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.620
to those cloud services very
quickly, and more importantly

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.690
become more productive
in composing your APIs

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:17.820
and through the leveraging
some of those cloud services.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:22.640
So as I mentioned, it's all
about acceleration and Apigee

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:23.660
extensions.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:25.340
As we will see them
today in a demo,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.860
it gives you all
the capabilities

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.990
to be able to leverage
those cloud services.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.980
So what are extensions?

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.880
We look at them in
three different ways.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.530
So first off, extensions
provide a well-vetted framework,

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:44.360
which gives the ability
for our customers

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:48.160
to be able to take a
policy-based approach

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:50.370
in accessing those
cloud services.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.970
So what that means is, of
course every cloud service is

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.625
going to have an API endpoint.

00:07:54.625 --> 00:07:56.000
But what we're
talking about here

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.320
is giving you as developers
access to those services using

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:01.910
policy-based controls.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:04.910
Number two, we give you
all the capabilities

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:08.280
so you can reuse your
extensions very rapidly

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.000
and avoid the error-prone--
the redundancy issue

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.260
that we spoke about.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:14.690
And last but not the
least, this is our gateway

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.320
into providing all
the new innovation.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.770
So as an example, as customers
adopt AI and ML in their APIs,

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.390
these extensions
give you the ability

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:26.430
to be able to tap into some
of those services very,

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.160
very quickly.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:31.590
So today, I'm very
pleased to announce

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:35.010
extensions-- general
availability of these five

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:39.150
extensions which take
our catalog to a much

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.120
higher number.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.110
So Cloud authentication,
Cloud Functions,

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:48.130
which give the ability to access
protected Cloud Functions,

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:52.430
Cloud Machine Learning
Engine, BigQuery, and then

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.520
we have really bumped up the
support for our Data Loss

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:57.800
Prevention extension which
we released last year.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.900
But now we've added
support for templates.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.000
If you have been around
in the conference,

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.580
we did demo some
of these extensions

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:09.230
during one other
session as well.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:12.820
So this brings the total
number of extensions to 16.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:16.000
And we haven't just stopped
at building extension only

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.890
for Google Cloud Services.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:21.810
If you've been attentive,
you've heard a lot of focus

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.360
on hybrid and multicloud.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.760
So today, we'll actually
showcase to you--

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.990
and we did in another
session before as well--

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:32.670
that we have gone and built
extensions to other cloud

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:33.730
services as well.

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:36.300
So our customers can
leverage the Apigee platform.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.940
No matter where your workloads
are on which cloud provider,

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:41.670
you're able to seamlessly
access those services as well.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:49.680
OK, with that, if you can
switch to the demo, please.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:50.180
Awesome.

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.880
So in this
demonstration, I'm going

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.590
to walk you through the day in
the life of an API developer.

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:00.350
So, this would be the
persona or the role

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.850
who are using the Apigee
API platform for building

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.320
all these APIs.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.580
And we'll walk you through how
to leverage extensions and use

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:13.990
these cloud services
to deliver to the APIs.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:16.530
So, I'm logged into
Apigee Edge out here.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:21.600
And this is the interface
that I see when I get in.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.820
So I'm logged in out here
as an API developer, right?

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.060
In this case, I'm going to
be jumping back and forth

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:30.570
between the API developer
persona and the API

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.380
administrator persona as well.

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:35.850
So first off, I
have a requirement

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:39.980
to now go build an API and
get some employee data.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.850
That data happens to
be sitting in BigQuery.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.610
Traditionally, if
I go into Apigee,

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:47.350
I can whip up an API proxy.

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:50.290
An API proxy is nothing but
a very simple construct.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:53.980
It gives you the ability
to be able to compose

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:56.200
a lot of interaction
with many services.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.260
You can use a lot of different
policies out of the box.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:02.140
So I could very easily--
let's say an example--

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:04.840
go into one of the APIs.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:07.930
I'll just look this
one up as an example,

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:11.610
and assuming the demo
gods are with us today,

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:14.600
as they should be.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:17.130
So within this
API proxy, you can

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:20.070
add a bunch of different
kinds of policies, right?

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:24.720
So I can use traffic management
policies, security, mediation

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:29.250
policies, and I can also use an
extension capability as well.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:30.890
So it's a very
extensible platform.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.990
So using a combination of
many of these policies,

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:34.640
I can access pretty
much any cloud

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.520
service or any other service
that's out there, right?

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.080
But that's something which we've
been doing for a long time.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.460
So let's now focus our attention
on how do the extensions come

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:47.100
in and really help that cost.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:48.980
So with that, I'll
actually first start

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:50.900
with looking at
BigQuery because I'm

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:53.570
going to show you how you're
going to use the BigQuery

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.660
extension to get data
in and out of BigQuery

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:58.100
through Apigee Edge.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:01.610
So in this case, I
switched over to BigQuery.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:05.870
And I have a data set
called "human_resources"

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.330
and a pretty obvious
table called "employees."

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:12.600
In this case, let me run
a quick query in BigQuery.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:18.900
And once I get the results
out here, assuming you

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:24.260
guys can see this, I have
about seven different entries

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:27.190
in there, OK?

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:30.240
Let me see if I can mess with
the resolution a little bit.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.340
OK, a little bit better, right?

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.840
So this is actual
data sitting in there.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:37.320
So now, let's go over
and see how we can enable

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:39.570
that experience within Apigee.

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:43.380
So the first thing you do
now is you go into this area

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:46.860
where we call Extensions.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.650
So the first step is
go into Extensions

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:51.660
and go Add an Extension.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:53.700
As you can see,
we have extensions

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:57.150
to a lot of the services
that I just spoke about.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.580
You can click on any of these.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:01.140
In this case, you
would typically

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.880
go and click on BigQuery.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:06.440
You give it a name
and a description.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.560
