00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:27.440
to the Global Payments
backend processors,

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:30.360
backend processors go
connect to the card brands,

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:33.150
the regular business
response comes back,

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:37.910
and the transaction is either
authorized or declined.

00:27:37.910 --> 00:27:40.510
So, the Apigee gateway
basically gives us

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:43.270
a very powerful
abstraction layer

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:45.640
to hide all the
different complexities

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:47.890
we have with our processors.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:48.720
Those are complex.

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:52.200
These are huge APIs.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:56.250
Like I said, it mediates
the complexity for us.

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:59.730
Obviously, we have a whole
set of security layer

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:00.660
on top of the gateway.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:03.330
We leverage a whole bunch
of security policies

00:28:03.330 --> 00:28:05.490
for the gateway
or of the gateway.

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:11.090
So, how does this journey begin?

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:14.420
Global Payments did
had API gateways.

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:20.000
We began our journey with
Apigee late December 2017.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:25.820
A year in it, we purchased the
licenses for US, EU, and AP,

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:29.300
giving us the global
presence of the API gateway

00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:32.000
so that we can basically accept
the transactions from all

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.400
around the world,
giving us speed

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:39.120
and reliability in a secure
and compliant manner.

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:42.710
55,000 calls in
introduction weekly.

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:43.730
It's growing.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:46.610
This is-- remember,
we are one year in.

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:49.620
We had the development, QA,
certification, production.

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:52.790
We have a bunch of APIs
already running in production,

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:57.270
and the volume is
growing as we speak.

00:28:57.270 --> 00:29:00.510
We had 100% uptime
since we launched.

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.310
No downtime.

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:06.420
And again, increasing
adoption across the world,

00:29:06.420 --> 00:29:10.440
meaning with this geocapability
or global presence

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:14.663
of the gateway, we now
can accept transactions

00:29:14.663 --> 00:29:16.080
from all the corners
of the world.

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:22.110
So, today, the focus would be--

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:23.790
as the focus is on
extension and how we

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.470
can accelerate the development.

00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:31.450
We at Global Payments do use the
Stackdriver Logging extension.

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:32.223
Why logging?

00:29:32.223 --> 00:29:33.390
Why we started with logging?

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:38.210
PCI requirements.

00:29:38.210 --> 00:29:41.840
We have to log every
transaction that

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:43.820
is hitting the
gateway, specifically

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:47.000
now as we are launching
transaction APIs, dispute

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:50.150
APIs, all of those, and
moving our internal APIs

00:29:50.150 --> 00:29:52.160
on the gateway as well.

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:58.060
Every transaction that hit
the gateway should be logged.

00:29:58.060 --> 00:30:01.330
It is in our security
and architecture

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:04.210
practice in Global Payments.

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:05.570
Audit requirements.

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:07.425
We do those from the
fintech industries.

00:30:07.425 --> 00:30:10.090
We run through PCI
compliance audit.

00:30:10.090 --> 00:30:13.330
We have to show the
evidences of the logs.

00:30:13.330 --> 00:30:15.250
How are we collecting the logs?

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:19.800
These logs are then fed
into GCP Stackdriver,

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:23.110
where we have configured
alerting and monitoring

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:25.100
of the logs.

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:26.890
You can navigate to
the issue faster.

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:29.645
All of these logs are
getting fed into Stackdriver

00:30:29.645 --> 00:30:30.270
we are alerted.

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:32.230
We are monitoring these logs.

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:35.560
We can navigate through the
logs with different search

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:39.310
parameters in the log
entry and get to this issue

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:41.680
faster in production
environment.

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:43.180
Consistent log-- very important.

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:45.710
I'm going to touch
on that real soon.

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:50.000
Every transaction that
hits the gateway is logged

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:52.040
in a consistent fashion--

00:30:52.040 --> 00:30:55.760
who, where, what, why, how.

00:30:55.760 --> 00:31:00.440
This is a consistent format
now across every API we have.

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:02.410
Traceability, of course, right?

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:05.450
We have full traceability by
capturing these parameters

00:31:05.450 --> 00:31:07.330
of who, where, what, and how.

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:15.010
So, this is how we started with.

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:18.580
We started with logging.

00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:21.580
We had to log every transaction
come in the gateway.

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:25.630
So this is how our
Apigee flow looked

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:28.190
without Apigee extensions.

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:31.370
We create the KVMs,
assign the project ID,

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:35.620
log ID, use a variable,
generate the GCP tokens,

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:41.710
use the private key, cache it,
and connect to the GCP project.

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:45.250
Invoke the Stackdriver
service, log it asynchronously

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:46.000
behind the scenes.

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:52.400
And this, if it hits--

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:53.720
there you go.

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:58.790
And this is how it is after
applying the extensions.

00:31:58.790 --> 00:31:59.780
Two steps.

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:03.680
You form the log message,
do an Extension Callout

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:06.080
to the Stackdriver
extension that we have

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:08.960
configured via our org admins.

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:10.930
That's it.

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:16.420
Now, the way we use this
extension is this extension

00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:20.770
is created in a shared flow and
is implemented at a flow hook

00:32:20.770 --> 00:32:21.940
level.

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:25.780
That means that any new proxy
I'm putting on the gateway--

00:32:25.780 --> 00:32:28.270
I do not have to worry whether
it is logging or not logging

00:32:28.270 --> 00:32:31.780
because, as you know,
if it is in a flow hook,

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:33.540
that is going to be executed.

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:35.000
That's it.

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:38.260
So now any proxy,
whether the developer

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:41.920
remembers to add log, add
extensions, use extensions,

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:46.000
regardless, every transaction
is now getting logged.

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:51.340
This has helped us tremendously
in delivering the proxies

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:55.180
in a right, compliant manner.

00:32:55.180 --> 00:32:57.880
I'm logging any proxy
that I put on the gateway.

00:32:57.880 --> 00:33:01.510
In fact, a couple of
minutes ago, we just

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:04.660
added one proxy which was
a high term priority for us

00:33:04.660 --> 00:33:05.930
in matter of hours.

00:33:05.930 --> 00:33:06.430
That's it.

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:08.097
Our proxy is out in
the dev environment.

00:33:08.097 --> 00:33:09.820
And very soon, it
will make its way

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:12.725
to the higher environments
of QA, certification, prod,

00:33:12.725 --> 00:33:14.700
and so.

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:20.370
So, we do look forward to use
more extensions as now we have

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:23.400
more announcements from Apigee.

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:25.410
DLP is definitely a
cool one, Prithpal.

00:33:25.410 --> 00:33:27.340
We definitely want
to look at that.

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:31.313
So we will be using or looking
into using more extensions

00:33:31.313 --> 00:33:31.980
at the platform.

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:36.990
Benefits.

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:37.490
So--

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:38.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:38.300 --> 00:33:39.160
Thanks.

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:41.053
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:41.053 --> 00:33:43.220
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: That was
a very powerful use case.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:44.540
No, let's stay up here.

00:33:44.540 --> 00:33:45.640
GOPIKA PATEL: Yeah.

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:48.140
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: So, thanks
for sharing this story, Gopika.

00:33:48.140 --> 00:33:50.810
I thought you walked us
through how you're just

00:33:50.810 --> 00:33:54.630
very early on in your journey
and are just getting started.

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:56.310
And I'm pretty sure
you guys are going

00:33:56.310 --> 00:33:59.900
to hit some very high
volumes very soon.

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:03.710
And we are prepared to
ensure that you folks scale

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:05.870
with your platform
by leveraging Apigee.

00:34:05.870 --> 00:34:08.014
And thanks for walking
us through why logging

00:34:08.014 --> 00:34:09.889
has become kind of this
new money or currency

00:34:09.889 --> 00:34:10.889
that we talk about.

00:34:10.889 --> 00:34:12.472
Because from a
compliance perspective,

00:34:12.472 --> 00:34:14.000
this becomes super important.

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:16.909
So, I just wanted to
kind of summarize.

00:34:16.909 --> 00:34:19.940
We saw a lot of things
happening today.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:21.980
We spoke about how
do we accelerate

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:25.670
development of these APIs
using cloud services.

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:28.710
We also looked at
some very routine ways

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:32.210
of doing that from the platform,
as we showcased with your--

00:34:32.210 --> 00:34:34.460
that comparison was pretty
awesome, by the way, right?

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:35.870
You can still use the platform.

00:34:35.870 --> 00:34:39.270
It's completely extensible
to do things like logging

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:41.078
to Stackdriver, as an example.

00:34:41.078 --> 00:34:42.620
But then you also
saw extensions were

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:45.889
able to very quickly
compartmentalize them and make

00:34:45.889 --> 00:34:47.020
it a one-two show, right?

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:47.770
GOPIKA PATEL: Yep.

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:49.219
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: The
administrators go and configure

00:34:49.219 --> 00:34:49.719
it.

00:34:49.719 --> 00:34:51.643
And the developers use
policy-based controls.

00:34:51.643 --> 00:34:53.060
So we were just
talking about this

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:56.239
when we-- before we came up as
to some of the new extension.

00:34:56.239 --> 00:34:58.440
I think the DLP extension,
as you mentioned,

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:01.610
especially the template
support, is becoming very, very

00:35:01.610 --> 00:35:04.520
important for customers who are
worried about data security.

00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:07.510
And the last piece that you
touched on, I want to re-echo.

00:35:07.510 --> 00:35:10.130
Gopika used a term
called "flow hook."

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:12.410
So, one thing which is super
important to understand

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:16.750
is compliance is not an option.

00:35:16.750 --> 00:35:19.090
A developer may be
very good at heart

00:35:19.090 --> 00:35:22.330
and may remember to do all the
right things such as putting

00:35:22.330 --> 00:35:23.780
in all those policies.

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:25.280
But what if they forget?

00:35:25.280 --> 00:35:27.430
Ultimately, the
responsibility is

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:30.640
with the program to ensure
that there is compliance.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:34.300
So flow hook is a mechanism
within the platform where

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:35.770
you can use a
shared flow, the one

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:38.620
that I call from
within my API proxy.

00:35:38.620 --> 00:35:43.360
The same shared flow can be
configured at an environment

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:47.530
level so it automatically
applies any policies out there

00:35:47.530 --> 00:35:50.620
for all API traffic going
through that environment, which

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:52.870
means from a compliance
perspective you can just

00:35:52.870 --> 00:35:55.810
add a shared flow with the
extension for DLP, which

00:35:55.810 --> 00:35:57.340
is what we demonstrated.

00:35:57.340 --> 00:36:00.755
And you are using Stackdriver
Logging in such a way as well.

00:36:00.755 --> 00:36:02.380
And the last thing
to add to that is we

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:04.900
also, with this
announcement, now

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:07.450
provide support for
a post-client flow.

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:09.880
So that means
typically a logging

00:36:09.880 --> 00:36:12.672
is a mechanism
which is important,

00:36:12.672 --> 00:36:15.130
but you also don't want it to
come in the way of processing

00:36:15.130 --> 00:36:16.130
that API call.

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:18.400
So you can now use
the shared flow,

00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:19.842
and that policy
more importantly,

00:36:19.842 --> 00:36:21.550
in a post-client flow,
which means it can

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:23.960
be done truly asynchronously.

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:29.890
So recapping that, first-class,
extensible, self-service

00:36:29.890 --> 00:36:32.530
approach to connect
to cloud services

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:35.650
using these pre-built
extensions very rapidly.

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:37.030
Connect to any service.

00:36:37.030 --> 00:36:40.150
We've already built 16 different
extensions that you saw--

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:43.450
12 to different Google
Cloud services, and some

00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:45.550
to other providers as well.

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:49.060
And we definitely plan
to continue growing

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:50.358
the catalog of extensions.

00:36:50.358 --> 00:36:52.150
The other thing that
we're working on today

00:36:52.150 --> 00:36:56.620
is the same framework and the
same development methodology

00:36:56.620 --> 00:36:59.740
that we, as the
Apigee team, are using

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:01.510
to build these extensions.

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.940
We are planning on
opening up that framework

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:08.880
to our own customers so then
you can take that framework,

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:11.710
build our own extensions,
your own services,

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:14.230
and reuse them within
the enterprise,

00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:17.170
just like you can from
the pre-built extensions.

00:37:17.170 --> 00:37:17.810
OK.

